            <ArticleTitle>Calcified thoracic intervertebral disc at two levels as a cause of mid-back pain in a child: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>587-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>An uncommon case of calcified disc at two levels in the thoracic spine is presented. A 12-year-old girl presented with intervertebral thoracic disc calcification from the levels D7-D8 and D11-D12. Level D7-D8 remained asymptomatic, whereas at level D11-D12 she had spontaneous pain for 10 months. Her symptoms were self-limiting and disappeared with bed rest, analgesics, and the use of a brace. The radiological changes were the same at 4-year follow-up, but the patient remained asymptomatic.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
